The diagram is intentionally logical. The exact foreign-key implementation, including nullable relationships and polymorphic targets, must follow the detailed table requirements below. `polls.post_id`, `posts.community_id`, and `communities.location_id` are nullable foreign keys (D-19). Polymorphic references (`notifications.entity_type`/`entity_id`, `reports.target_type`/`target_id`, `moderation_actions.target_type`/`target_id`) are shown in the table specifications, not as ERD links: PostgreSQL cannot provide an ordinary foreign key across multiple target tables, so these references are validated in trusted application logic (see §4.6.1).

## 3.1 Relationship rules

1. A profile belongs to one authenticated Supabase user.
2. A user may create many posts.
3. A post may receive many reactions and comments.
4. A poll contains multiple options.
5. A poll may receive multiple votes.
6. A user may have one active vote submission per poll, subject to the approved poll rules.
7. A game contains questions.
8. A game session belongs to one user and one game.
9. A game session contains answers.
10. A community has many members.
11. A user may belong to multiple communities.
12. A report is submitted by a user and may identify a target user or content record.
13. A report may have one or more moderation actions.
14. Notifications belong to their intended recipient.
15. Administrative and moderation actions must have auditable actors.
16. A user has at most one active role row in `user_roles` at a time (D-18).
17. A poll may be linked to a post through nullable `polls.post_id`; whether polls are always linked or may exist independently is TBD (DB-OD-004 — see README Open Decisions register; do not invent).
18. A post may belong to a community through nullable `posts.community_id`, and a community may reference a location through nullable `communities.location_id`.

All relationships must be implemented with appropriate foreign keys or explicitly documented alternatives.

