# 5. ENUMS AND CONTROLLED VALUES

Use PostgreSQL enums or validated reference tables where they provide a clear benefit.

Do not create excessive enums for values likely to change frequently.

Candidate controlled values:

| Domain | Values |
|---|---|
| Profile visibility | public, private |
| Account status | active, restricted, suspended, pending_deletion, deleted |
| Post status | published, hidden, removed, deleted |
| Reaction type | like, funny, interesting, surprising |
| Poll selection | single, multiple |
| Poll status | draft, active, closed, removed |
| Game session status | active, completed, abandoned, expired |
| Community membership | active, pending, left, removed |
| Report status | open, under_review, action_taken, dismissed, escalated |
| Location source | manual, approximate_browser, system_assigned |
| User role | registered, moderator, administrator, super_administrator |

Enum-vs-text is TBD (owner Dennis, Product P0 — see README Open Decisions register). Do not invent the decision, and do not create enums for values likely to change frequently. There is no existing database to inspect (D-04).

Avoid adding enum values in a way that creates deployment-order or transaction compatibility problems.

